      "synth": {
        "answer": "### Van Inwagen’s Consequence Argument\n\nPeter van Inwagen presents the **Consequence Argument** as \"the best argument\" for incompatibilism—the view that free will and causal determinism are incompatible [Source: incompatibilism-arguments]. The argument is designed to show that if determinism is true, then no one has any power to alter their actions or choices.\n\nThe core of the argument can be reconstructed in three steps (often formalized with a modal operator 'N', meaning \"no one has ability/power over\"):\n\n1.  **The Past and Laws:** No one has power over the past (facts about what happened before we were born) and no one has power over the laws of nature.\n2.  **Deterministic Entailment:** If determinism is true, then our present actions are unavoidable consequences of the past and the laws [Source: compatibilism]. Specifically, given the past ($P$) and the laws ($L$), only one future is possible; therefore, if $P \\& L$, it follows that action $A$ occurs.\n3.  **Transfer of Powerlessness:** Since no one has power over $P$ or $L$, and since $P \\& L$ entails $A$, it follows that no one has power over whether $A$ occurs (or [unverified excerpt: no one can do otherwise than they actually do]) [Source: moral-responsibility][Source: freewill].\n\nVan Inwagen argues that this conclusion—that we are never able to do anything other than what we actually do—undermines the sort of free will required for moral responsibility [Source: incompatibilism-arguments]. He insists this is not a verbal dispute about the meaning of 'can'; rather, he claims both compatibilists and incompatibilists use 'is able' in exactly the same way as ordinary English speakers during deliberation. Therefore, if determinism implies no ability to do otherwise, then determinism rules out free will [Source: incompatibilism-arguments].\n\n### The Strongest Compatibilist Response: David Lewis’s \"Two Senses\" Objection\n\nThe most influential objection to van Inwagen's argument comes from **David Lewis (1981)**, who argues that the Consequence Argument relies on an equivocation regarding what it means for an agent to be \"able\" to break a law of nature or alter the past [Source: freewill][Source: abilities].\n\nLewis distinguishes between two distinct senses in which one might claim to have the power to violate a deterministic consequence:\n\n**1. Ability to Break a Law (\"Small Miracle\")**\nIn this sense, having the ability to do otherwise would require performing a \"small miracle\"—a violation of the actual laws of nature at the moment of choice. For example, if I choose not to raise my hand when determinism dictates I will, doing so would mean breaking a physical law right then and there. Lewis argues that no human being has this kind of supernatural power; we cannot break the laws of physics [Source: freewill][Source: abilities]. Under *this* definition, van Inwagen is correct: we are unable to do otherwise because it requires violating natural laws.\n\n**2. Ability to Act Otherwise (\"Counterfactual Strength\")**\nIn this second sense, having the ability to do otherwise simply means acting in accordance with one's own desires, character, or decision-making processes without external coercion. If I had chosen differently (or if my internal deliberative process had resulted in a different output), I would have acted differently. This does not require breaking any physical law; rather, it relies on counterfactual conditionals that remain true even within a deterministic framework [Source: freewill][Source: abilities]. For instance, [unverified excerpt: If I had wanted to raise my hand instead of keeping it down, I would have raised it.]\n\n### Why Lewis’s Response is Strongest\nLewis’s response effectively neutralizes the Consequence Argument by showing its second premise (\"no one has power over whether A occurs\") only holds if we accept van Inwagen's first definition (the ability to break laws). However, compatibilists argue that moral responsibility and free will do *not* require the supernatural power to violate physical laws. They only require the ordinary capacity for reasons-responsiveness—the ability to act according to one's own motivations [Source: freewill][Source: abilities]. By separating these two senses, Lewis demonstrates that determinism does not eliminate the kind of \"power\" relevant to human agency; it merely eliminates a physically impossible, god-like power that no one actually possesses or needs for freedom [Source: incompatibilism-arguments][Source: freewill].\n\n### Other Compatibilist Strategies\nWhile Lewis’s semantic distinction is the most direct rebuttal to van Inwagen’s logic, other compatibilists have adopted different strategies:\n*   **Rejecting PAP (Principle of Alternative Possibilities):** Some modern compatibilists argue they do not need an ability to do otherwise at all. They reject the premise that free will requires regulative control (the power to bring about X *and* refrain from bringing about X), focusing instead on whether actions stem from the agent's own values and reasons-responsiveness [Source: alternative-possibilities][Source: compatibilism].\n*   **New Dispositionalism:** Other theorists attempt to explain how agents can possess a genuine \"disposition\" or capacity to act differently in a deterministic world, arguing that such capacities are compatible with fixed laws if understood as internal traits rather than external miracles [Source: compatibilism].\n\nHowever, Lewis’s critique remains central because it directly addresses van Inwagen’s claim that determinists and incompatibilists disagree over ordinary language. Van Inwagen counters Lewis by insisting there is only one relevant sense of 'can'—the ability to do otherwise given the past and laws—and he rejects Lewis's distinction as muddling the debate [Source: incompatibilism-arguments].\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- Lewis distinguishes between two distinct senses in which one might claim to have the power to violate a deterministic consequence\n- Under *this* definition, van Inwagen is correct: we are unable to do otherwise because it requires violating natural laws",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
