      "synth": {
        "answer": "David Hume and Immanuel Kant offer fundamentally different accounts of causation, rooted in their opposing views on the source of human knowledge. For Hume, causality is a psychological habit derived from experience; for Kant, it is an *a priori* category of understanding that structures experience itself.\n\n### 1. Hume’s Account: Causation as Custom\nHume’s analysis begins with his \"fork,\" which divides all objects of human reason into two categories: **relations of ideas** (analytic propositions known through logic) and **matters of fact** (contingent truths known only through sense experience). [Source: hume] Because causal connections are not logical necessities—one can always conceive of a cause without its effect—they cannot be relations of ideas. Therefore, any knowledge of causality must come from matters of fact. [Source: induction-problem]\n\nHowever, when we examine our sensory impressions, we never perceive the \"necessary connection\" or \"glue\" between events; we observe only one event following another in constant conjunction. [Source: causation-law] Hume argues that because no amount of observation reveals an inherent power linking cause to effect, the belief in causality is not based on reason but on **custom** or habit. After repeatedly observing Event A followed by Event B, the mind forms a customary association such that the appearance of A immediately leads us to expect B. Thus, for Hume, causality is a subjective projection of the human imagination onto the world, grounded entirely in empirical experience and associative psychology. [Source: hegel-dialectics]; [Source: hume]\n\n### 2. Kant’s Account: Causation as a Transcendental Category\nKant famously stated that reading Hume \"awoke him from his dogmatic slumbers.\" [Source: hume] He agreed with Hume that causal necessity cannot be derived *from* experience (since experience only yields contingent observations). However, Kant rejected Hume's conclusion that causality was merely a subjective habit. Instead, Kant argued that if we have universal and necessary knowledge of causes (as seen in Newtonian physics), this knowledge must be **synthetic *a priori***—it adds to our knowledge but is independent of specific experiences. [Source: induction-problem]; [Source: sep]\n\nTo explain how such knowledge is possible, Kant proposed a \"Copernican revolution\": rather than assuming our mind conforms to objects, we must assume that objects conform to the structure of our mind. [Source: hegel-dialectics] Causality is one of the pure concepts or **\"categories\" of the understanding**. It is not something we discover in nature; it is a rule imposed by the human cognitive faculty upon sensory data to make experience coherent. Without the category of causation, our perceptions would remain a chaotic stream of subjective associations (what Hume called \"custom\") and could never be organized into objective events with strict universality. [Source: kant-transcendental]; [Source: sep]\n\n### 3. Why Kant Saw a Problem Demanding a Transcendental Solution\nKant took Hume’s skepticism seriously because he believed Hume had correctly identified that **empiricism alone cannot justify scientific necessity.** If knowledge were limited strictly to what sense experience provides—as Hume argued—then universal laws like \"every event has a cause\" would lack justification. They would be mere generalizations based on past observations, offering no guarantee for the future. This leads to radical skepticism about science and metaphysics. [Source: induction-problem]; [Source: hegel-dialectics]\n\nFor Kant, this was unacceptable because it threatened the very foundation of rational inquiry. He needed to explain why our ordering of representations is **universal and necessary** rather than merely contingent or habitual. [Source: kant-transcendental] The Humean proposal—that association alone could account for causality—failed because simple sensory associations (like word association) do not exhibit the requisite universality; they are subjective and variable. [Source: kant-transcendental] Therefore, a \"transcendental solution\" was required: one that demonstrated how the mind’s innate structures (the categories) make objective experience possible in the first place. By grounding causality in the transcendental structure of human reason rather than empirical habit, Kant aimed to secure scientific knowledge against skeptical doubt while acknowledging its limits to the realm of possible experience. [Source: hegel-dialectics]; [Source: sep]\n\nI don't have reliable information on whether there were specific contemporary critiques of this synthesis from figures like Jacobi or Maimon beyond what is briefly noted; would you like me to search for more details on those objections?\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- Because causal connections are not logical necessities—one can always conceive of a cause without its effect—they cannot be relations of ideas.\n- of the understanding. It is not something we discover in nature; it is a rule imposed by the human cognitive faculty upon sensory data to make experience cohere…",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
