      "vector_eligible": true,
      "synth": {
        "answer": "Aristotle’s hylomorphic account of substance posits that physical objects are compounds (*synolon*) of matter and form. Matter is the potentiality to become a specific thing, while form is the actuality or essence that makes it what it is [Source: aristotle-metaphysics]. Form satisfies Aristotle's criteria for primary substance because it provides unity, separability (in definition), and intelligibility [Source: aristotle-metaphysics], whereas matter alone fails to be a determinate \"this something\" (*tode ti*) [Source: substance].\n\nIn *De Anima*, Aristotle applies this framework to living beings by defining the soul as the \"substance as form of a natural body which has life in potentiality\" (412a20–1) [Source: aristotle-psychology]. The soul is not a separate entity but the first actuality or organizing principle that accounts for all vital functions—nutrition, perception, and thought—in an organism [Source: ancient-soul; Source: medieval-philosophy]. This view steers a middle course between Platonic dualism (which treats the soul as separable from the body) and reductive materialism (which reduces the soul to mere matter), asserting instead that the ensouled body is a unified hylomorphic compound [Source: aristotle-psychology].\n\nCommentators disagree on whether this account constitutes non-reductive form-of-the-body unity or a kind of property dualism. The \"non-reductive\" reading emphasizes Aristotle's insistence that the soul is inseparable from the body in most respects, just as shape cannot exist without bronze [Source: dualism; Source: aristotle-psychology]. However, other commentators point to passages where Aristotle suggests certain parts of the intellect (*nous*) are separable and immortal because they lack a bodily organ (De Anima 413a6–7) [Source: dualism; Source: byzantine-philosophy]. This exception allows for an interpretation akin to property dualism—where mental properties (specifically rational thought) appear irreducible to physical processes—or even substance dualism regarding the active intellect. Thus, while hylomorphism generally unifies soul and body, the unique status granted to *nous* creates tension that some interpret as a form of dualist \"leakage\" within an otherwise unified system [Source: aristotle-psychology; Source: medieval-philosophy].\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- Commentators disagree on whether this account constitutes non-reductive form-of-the-body unity or a kind of property dualism.",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
        "stream_wall_ms": 51980,
